+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: bornaradusin <bornaradusin@users.noreply.github.com>
+Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH] xlive: Games for Windows - LIVE offline, so the games made
+ for it start.
+
+A game made for Games for Windows - LIVE imports xlive.dll, by ordinal,
+and Windows' loader refuses it without the library: Resident Evil 5's
+retail RE5DX10.EXE stopped at once, "Library xlive.dll ... not found"
+(c0000135). GTA IV before its Complete Edition, Fallout 3, Batman: Arkham
+Asylum, Dark Souls: Prepare to Die Edition, Street Fighter IV and
+BioShock 2 are such games too.
+
+This is a builtin xlive.dll with all 252 of xlive 3.5's ordinals, which
+answers as Live does for a profile signed in locally: user 0 is signed in
+locally (eXUserSigninState_SignedInLocally) as "Player" with a fixed
+offline XUID, as a game needs a signed-in profile to save. Its hooks
+(XLiveInitialize, XLiveInput, XLiveRender, the device calls) succeed and
+leave the game its input; protected buffers are plain memory and
+protected data is passed through as it is; profile settings a game writes
+are kept in the registry, under HKCU\Software\Wine\xlive\<program>;
+achievements are taken and forgotten, enumerations are empty, and what
+needs Live fails with ERROR_NOT_LOGGED_ON. Asynchronous calls complete at
+once. The XSocket and XWSA calls are Winsock's, a VDP socket being UDP;
+the XNet ones answer for the loopback address.
+
+The ordinals and names are those GFWL replacements publish
+(XLiveLessNess' xlive_exports.def); the code is new. Three functions whose
+arguments aren't known stay stubs. A game's own xlive.dll (xliveless, or
+XLiveLessNess) still loads first: the module prefers a native one.
